(13448,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3ACabbage_Kimchi','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Cabbage Kimchi','korean','side',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,77.14,4.22,0.53,0.1,16.17,3.87,3.55,13812.96,'B',60,0.8,3,'Korean radishes are large (about ½ to 1 kilogram). Japanese (daikon) radishes or a larger quantity of small European radishes could substitute.
The brine can be varied to include many other ingredients. Be creative.
The aekjeot can be replaced with moderate amounts of saeujeot (Korean salted tiny shrimp), raw butterfish, or raw oysters. Seafood can also be left out entirely, although an appropriate amount of salt needs to be added in place of missing jeot gal. Alternative flavors can include Asian pears or tangerines.','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Gimchi.jpg/960px-Gimchi.jpg',640,480,'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gimchi.jpg','Nagyman, a flickr user','CC BY-SA 2.0',10),
(14851,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3ALemon_Meringue_Pie_(British)','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Lemon Meringue Pie (British)',NULL,'dessert',NULL,NULL,4,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0.4286,3,'There are 2 types of lemon meringue pie. The more common version has a lemon "gel" in the centre (which is often a luminous yellow colour), and a hard pastry base. The version above is much stickier.
In an earlier version, the filling used 3 egg yolks. It isn''t necessary.
When making the middle, it''s essential to mix in the egg before the lemon. The acidity of the lemon acts on the yolk protein to thicken it; putting in the lemon first (which is tempting if you want to "add lemon to taste" without eating raw egg yolk) will cause the yolk to go "stringy" on contact.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,7),
(21102,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3ABasic_Mayonnaise','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Basic Mayonnaise',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,0,1454.64,5.47,169.21,133.66,2.57,0.31,0.17,643.8,'D',36,1,3,'One can make the whisking process easier by using a kitchen towel. Wet a kitchen towel, or any small towel, until it is very wet and heavy, but not dripping. Coil the towel on top of a flat surface, such as the kitchen countertop or a table. Place the bowl onto the circle.  This will provide a steady base for the bowl and allow you to whisk with one hand while adding ingredients with the other.
If the mayonnaise curdles, it can be recovered by slowly dribbling the curdled mayonnaise into another egg yolk while whisking vigorously the whole time.
½ teaspoon white sugar can be added with the salt at the beginning for those with a sweet tooth.
It''s easier to just use an immersion blender for the whisking.
For special occasion, you can spice up this recipe by making Juanz mayonnaise: Just add about a handful of finely chopped cilantro and about half a tooth of crushed garlic. This is also known as banana''s mayonnaise.
Fry sauce is 2 parts of mayonnaise mixed with 1 part of tomato ketchup. It goes well with fries.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,3),
(21751,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3AFudge_Frosting','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Fudge Frosting',NULL,NULL,NULL,15,10,0,303.34,0.49,4.91,2.91,65.64,63.73,0.46,33.39,'D',38,1,2,'A very small amount of milk may be added if the mixture is unspreadable even while hot. Don''t add more than you truly need.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,5),
